\name{stats_fit}
\alias{stats_fit}
\title{Fit one linear model per feature and test coefficients with limma's moderated t}
\usage{
stats_fit(responses, design, coefficients, trend = FALSE, spline_basis = NULL,
  threads = 1, timeout = 600)
}
\arguments{
\item{responses}{A TSV with a header row. First column the feature id; every other column one
  sample, named by its header. Use the values as you want them modelled - log2 intensities,
  usually. Blank, \code{NA} or \code{NaN} is missing and left out of that feature's fit, never imputed;
  \strong{0 is an observation}, so blank out any 0 that means "not measured".}

\item{design}{A TSV with a header row and one row per sample: first column the sample name
  (matching the responses header exactly, in any order), then one numeric column per
  coefficient. Include an intercept column yourself if you want one.}

\item{coefficients}{Design column names to test, each with its own moderated test and its own
  Benjamini-Hochberg family.}

\item{trend}{Let the prior variance follow average response (limma's \code{trend = TRUE}), for data
  whose low-abundance features are noisier.}

\item{spline_basis}{Basis functions of the trend spline, intercept included (the bridge's
  default is 4). Only with \code{trend = TRUE}; mzLib may use fewer, and \code{prior$spline_basis_count}
  reports what it used.}

\item{threads}{Features fitted at once (threads), or \code{-1} for every core. The answer is identical
  at any value.}

\item{timeout}{Seconds to allow, or \code{NULL} to wait indefinitely.
}

}
\description{
Fit one linear model per feature of a feature-by-sample table and test each named coefficient with limma's empirical-Bayes moderated t, Benjamini-Hochberg adjusted, in one call.

Runs mzLib's \code{LinearModel.Fit} once - every design column is fitted - and then
\code{EmpiricalBayes.Moderate} for each coefficient you name, which also applies Benjamini-Hochberg
across that coefficient's fitted features. This is limma's \code{lmFit()} then
\code{eBayes(legacy = TRUE)} (Smyth 2004), which mzLib holds to limma to 1e-8 relative on limma's own
reference output.
}
\details{
A feature is fitted on the samples where it was observed, and is reported - never dropped - when
it cannot be: \code{"too_few_observations"} when it has no more observed samples than the design has
coefficients, \code{"rank_deficient"} when its observed samples cannot separate the coefficients (a
feature seen in only one group cannot have a group effect).
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_moderated_fit}:

  - \code{records}, a data.frame with one row per (tested coefficient, feature) - one block of
    \code{feature_count} rows per coefficient, in the order you named them - with \code{feature},
    \code{coefficient}, \code{status}, \code{estimate} (response units per coefficient unit: a \strong{log2 fold
    change} for a 0/1 group column on log2 intensities), \code{standard_error} (response units per
    coefficient unit), \code{t} (standard errors), \code{df_total} (degrees of freedom), \code{p_value} and
    \code{bh_adjusted} (fractions, 0 to 1), \code{posterior_variance} and \code{prior_variance} (squared
    response units), \code{sigma} (response units), \code{df_residual}, \code{observed} (samples) and
    \code{average_response} (response units). Statistics are \code{NA} where \code{status} is not \code{"fitted"}.
  - \code{feature_count} features and \code{sample_count} samples read; \code{sample_names},
    \code{coefficient_names} (every design column) and \code{tested_coefficients}; \code{missing_count} and
    \code{zero_count} response cells; \code{status_counts}, features per status; \code{threads}; \code{row_count}
    rows.
  - \code{residual_df_differ}: \code{TRUE} when the fitted features do not all have the same residual
    degrees of freedom. Then default limma (3.61 and later) uses a different prior estimator and
    reports different moderated statistics; these are \code{legacy = TRUE}.
  - \code{prior}, the fitted variance prior, shared by every coefficient: \code{df} (degrees of freedom,
    \code{NA} when infinite), \code{df_infinite}, \code{trended}, \code{spline_basis_count} (basis functions) and
    \code{scale} (squared response units, \code{NA} when trended).
  - \code{caveats}, including any that apply to this input.

}
\section{What it is not}{


It is limma's *legacy* estimator. \code{robust = TRUE}, contrasts, the B-statistic and observation
weights are not implemented; write a contrast as its own design column instead. \code{bh_adjusted}
controls the false discovery rate among the features tested; it is not a target-decoy q-value.

}
